  "overview": {
    "claims": [
      {
        "attribution": "Supreme Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-54d12418d20d-0000-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 1,
              "start": 1
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Oral argument in this case began at 11:05\u202fa.m. before the Supreme Court of the United States."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Petitioner (Paul M. Rashkind)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-54d12418d20d-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Petitioner (Paul M. Rashkind), according to Petitioner (Paul M. Rashkind), the petitioner, represented by Paul\u202fM.\u202fRashkind, argues that the Eleventh Circuit\u2019s categorical prohibition of Rule\u202f60(b) in habeas corpus cases absent fraud is contrary to the approach of most other circuits and to the United States\u2019 amicus brief filed in this case."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Rashkind",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-54d12418d20d-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 19,
              "start": 19
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Mr. Rashkind, rule\u202f60(b) provides a disciplined, limited approach with six specific grounds and is not a \u201cwide\u2011open door.\u201d"
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Rashkind",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-54d12418d20d-0001-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 20,
              "start": 20
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Mr. Rashkind, under AEDPA, a person who has exhausted state\u2011court claims, has not procedurally defaulted, and has filed a timely petition under 28\u202fU.S.C.\u202f\u00a7\u202f2254 is entitled to have the claim entertained."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Petitioner (Paul M. Rashkind)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-54d12418d20d-0000-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 5,
              "start": 5
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Since the passage of AEDPA, only twenty\u2011eight successful motions of the type at issue have been quantified and published."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Petitioner",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-54d12418d20d-0003-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 53,
              "start": 53
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Petitioner, the first state habeas petition was dismissed because it was not notarized, which was the sole basis for dismissal."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Ms. Millett",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-54d12418d20d-0002-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 44,
              "start": 44
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Ms. Millett, amicus Ms.\u202fMillett argues that a Rule\u202f60(b) motion seeking to set aside a final judgment denying federal habeas relief on the ground that it was incorrectly decided is a second or successive petition under AEDPA and therefore must proceed under AEDPA\u2019s terms."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Ms. Millett",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-54d12418d20d-0002-0006"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 46,
              "start": 46
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Ms. Millett, ms.\u202fMillett further states that the vast majority of matters covered by Rule\u202f60(b) do not involve fraud or a biased judge."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Ms. Millett",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "54d12418d20dc1b6b09319c2afcd4d4397b7e5203125fe2b1b5bb1f5c893a3c6",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-54d12418d20d-0002-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 43,
              "start": 43
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Ms. Millett, ms.\u202fMillett says her position is only marginally less strict than the State of Florida\u2019s."
      }
    ],
